            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

The majority of air leakage is from the aluminum slider windows, they just can't make a tight enough seal. Replacement of the windows would have the most benefit. 



The weather stripping around the doors should also be replaced if it isn't making a tight seal. The door frame for the front door also had some air leakage. The trim can be removed from door and window frames and a special spray foam can be filled into the gap between the door jamb. In this case, the window frames seemed fine.



Another spot with air leakage is around the outlets and switches on exterior walls. This is another spot where spray foam can be used. If there is enough gap between the electrical box and the drywall, you can spray foam around the outside of the box. You can also get foam gaskets to go under the outlet plates as additional air leakage protection.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1300&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;45 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 10 inches (R32) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 190&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with minimal insulation. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1895&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;66 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</foundation>
                <windows>

The majority of your windows are older single pane, aluminum frame, sliders, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing 15 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1825&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;63 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <doors>

As your patio door is older, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your patio door with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 250&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new patio doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You could consider replacing your oil-fired hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings:

Electricity - $18 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $1.02 per litre 

Propane - $0.57 per litre

Wood - $350 per cord 

Pellets - $7 per bag



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



